  "question": "For $$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\quad B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},$$ verify that $A(B+C)=AB+AC$.",
  "answeroption": "A",
  "explanation": "<b>Addition before multiplication</b><br>$$B+C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$$<br>Therefore, $$A(B+C)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 13\\end{bmatrix}$$<br><b>Separate products</b><br>$$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\quad AC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$$<br>Hence, $$AB+AC=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 13\\end{bmatrix}$$<br><b>Distributive property</b><br>Thus, $A(B+C)=AB+AC$.",
  "explanation_image": [],
